Entry 2 — Status Update System (Marking Work as Late or Submitted)
Design Issue
Mr Marshall previously had to manually update student submission status in Canvas, which led to forgotten updates and inaccurate records.
Design Principles Applied
•	Feedback
•	Consistency
•	Simplicity
UX Characteristics
•	Usability
•	Error prevention
Design Change
I added a one-click status update system:
•	Buttons: “Mark Late” and “Mark Submitted”
•	Immediate visual confirmation after action (e.g. colour change + status label update)
•	Status changes are consistent across all views
Impact on User Experience
This reduces human error and ensures updates are not forgotten. The system provides clear feedback so Mr Marshall knows the action has worked, improving confidence and accuracy.
________________________________________
Entry 3 — Filtering and Search Function
Design Issue
With many students and assignments, it was difficult to find specific students or filter only missing/late work.
Design Principles Applied
•	Proximity
•	Repetition
•	Consistency
UX Characteristics
•	Learnability
•	Efficiency
Design Change
I implemented:
•	Filter buttons: “All”, “Missing”, “Late”, “Submitted”
•	Search bar for student names
•	Consistent layout of filtered results
Filters are placed close to the dataset to support proximity and reduce cognitive load.
Impact on User Experience
Mr Marshall can quickly isolate problem areas (e.g. only missing work), making the system faster and easier to learn and use.
________________________________________
Entry 4 — Colour Coding Status System
Design Issue
Without clear visual cues, it was hard to distinguish between submission statuses at a glance.
Design Principles Applied
•	Contrast
•	Colour theory
•	Accessibility
UX Characteristics
•	Usability
•	Accessibility
Design Change
I introduced a colour-coded system:
•	Red = Missing
•	Orange = Late
•	Green = Submitted
Colours were chosen with strong contrast against the background to ensure readability.
Impact on User Experience
Mr Marshall can now interpret the status of all students instantly without reading every label, improving speed and reducing cognitive load.
